    "body": "Great on any Friday: 70mai M800 4K Dash Cam - Front+Rear, Voice Control, Parking Monitor\nNASA Needs a Nuclear Reactor for the Moon, Now Looking for Someone to Build It\n9 photos\nPhoto: NASA\nI think it's pretty obvious to anyone looking that the size and scope of NASA's plans for the Moon will need a lot more than just batteries and solar panels to sustain themselves. Luckily, there is one more solution the agency can turn to: nuclear reactors.\nHaving already proven their merits here on Earth, nuclear reactors might just as well do the same on the lunar surface, acting as a source of electricity. If only there were someone able and willing to build one...\nIn the bid to find an answer to that question, the American space agency announced earlier this week that it is looking for industry partners to work on such a design. We don't have all the details of what is actually required, but the little bits of info that have been presented already are quite interesting.\nWithout going full-reveal on specifics, NASA said it requires a small fission power system capable of generating 20 kilowatts of electricity. On the Moon, that should be enough to feed power to future habitats, communications systems, rovers, scientific instruments, and whatever other infrastructure will be built up there.\nFor reference, 20 kilowatts of power is enough here on Earth to keep 16 average households afloat for an entire year. A mighty reactor, then, which could prove invaluable for the needs of building the Moon base.\nThe agency calls the design the Lunar Reactor-1 (LR-1), and intends to launch it to the Moon as soon as possible. Once delivered to the lunar surface, it should be able to operate without human intervention for as much as five years.\nAnd it could do that even during the lunar night, which lasts for two weeks on the Moon, seeing how it doesn't need solar power to do its thing. NASA is accepting proposals on the reactor by December 21, and says it could issue additional task orders for other nuclear power systems that it might require to fulfill its needs.\nIn fact, if you really think about it, NASA has already done so when it announced the Space Reactor-1 (SR-1) Freedom in late October. The name was slapped on both a spacecraft meant to deliver a trio of exploration helicopters collectively called SkyFall to Mars, and the reactor that powers it.\nNASA didn't provide us with all the details of the SR-1, but we're dealing with a system that generates heat through nuclear fission and, thanks to a conversion system, turns that heat into electricity that's then fed to the ship's power systems and electric propulsion.\nWhen it gets going, the Freedom will become the first fission-powered spacecraft to venture beyond Earth's orbit. The mission, which is expected to get going sometime in 2028, will also be used to inform the design of the Lunar Reactor-1.\nLiked the article? Follow us to see more stories from autoevolution in your Google feeds.\nEditor's note: Gallery shows the SkyFall helicopters that will be delivered to Mars using Space Reactor-1.\nNASA Lunar Reactor-1 lr-1 nuclaar\nAbout the author: Daniel Patrascu\nDaniel loves writing (or so he claims), and he uses this skill to offer readers a \"behind the scenes\" look at the automotive industry. He also enjoys talking about space exploration and robots, because in his view the only way forward for humanity is away from this planet, in metal bodies. Full profile\nUP NEXT Harley-Davidson Sends Three Fresh ST Baggers Our Way, Road King and More Are Back for 2027",
